ierre-Jacques Volaire","thumb":"https://www.artic.edu/iiif/2/6a676ee7-39c0-0b88-c37e-36ba0c5e3065/full/!240,240/0/default.jpg","state":"seen"},{"id":"aic:109938","title":"Valley of Aosta: Snowstorm, Avalanche, and Thunderstorm","artist":"Joseph Mallord William Turner","thumb":"https://www.artic.edu/iiif/2/564e2e3f-eb93-88a7-d265-8fea006facff/full/!240,240/0/default.jpg","state":"failed"},{"id":"mia:9612","title":"Rainstorm beneath the Summit","artist":"Katsushika Hokusai; Publisher: Nishimuraya Yohachi","thumb":"https://img.artsmia.org/web_objects_cache/009000/600/10/9612/mia_7021688_800.jpg","state":"seen"}]},"at":18127,"doneAt":18958},{"type":"data-step","id":"step-mug4561y-14","data":{"kind":"exhibit","phase":"done","startedAt":1790289498598,"endedAt":1790289498599,"count":6},"at":27808,"doneAt":27808},{"type":"data-exhibit","id":"exhibit-mug4561z-15","data":{"title":"The sky at full volume","note":"Church's sunset wins because it is the only sky here that fills most of the canvas, commits to one color and never blinks. Most of the runners-up are over the top in a different key: fire from below, weather as pure paint, a lightning bolt as graphic design, and one small pastel that manages the same drama in a few minutes.","artworks":[{"id":"cma:141639","source":"cma","nativeId":"141639","title":"Twilight in the Wilderness","artist":"Frederic Edwin Church","date":"1860","imageThumb":"https://openaccess-cdn.clevelandart.org/1965.233/1965.233_web.jpg","imageHires":"https://openaccess-cdn.clevelandart.org/1965.233/1965.233_print.jpg","license":"CC0","sourceUrl":"https://clevelandart.org/art/1965.233","accession":"1965.233","medium":"oil on canvas","dims":{"width":3400,"height":2123}},{"id":"aic:57996","source":"aic","nativeId":"57996","title":"The Eruption of Vesuvius","artist":"Pierre-Jacques Volaire","date":"1771","imageThumb":"https://www.artic.edu/iiif/2/6a676ee7-39c0-0b88-c37e-36ba0c5e3065/full/843,/0/default.jpg","imageHires":"https://www.artic.edu/iiif/2/6a676ee7-39c0-0b88-c37e-36ba0c5e3065/full/1686,/0/default.jpg","license":"CC0","sourceUrl":"https://www.artic.edu/artworks/57996","accession":"1978.426","medium":"Oil on canvas","dims":{"width":8600,"height":3958},"color":{"h":17,"s":83,"l":49}},{"id":"aic:109938","source":"aic","nativeId":"109938","title":"Valley of Aosta: Snowstorm, Avalanche, and Thunderstorm","artist":"Joseph Mallord William Turner","date":"1836–37","imageThumb":"https://www.artic.edu/iiif/2/564e2e3f-eb93-88a7-d265-8fea006facff/full/843,/0/default.jpg","imageHires":"https://www.artic.edu/iiif/2/564e2e3f-eb93-88a7-d265-8fea006facff/full/1686,/0/default.jpg","license":"CC0","sourceUrl":"https://www.artic.edu/artworks/109938","accession":"1947.513","medium":"Oil on canvas","dims":{"width":4914,"height":3665},"color":{"h":26,"s":0,"l":46}},{"id":"cma:150968","source":"cma","nativeId":"150968","title":"Fir Trees and Storm Clouds","artist":"Albert Bierstadt","date":"c. 1870","imageThumb":"https://openaccess-cdn.clevelandart.org/1982.37/1982.37_web.jpg","imageHires":"https://openaccess-cdn.clevelandart.org/1982.37/1982.37_print.jpg","license":"CC0","sourceUrl":"https://clevelandart.org/art/1982.37","accession":"1982.37","medium":"oil on paper mounted on canvas","dims":{"width":3400,"height":2490}},{"id":"mia:9612","source":"mia","nativeId":"9612","title":"Rainstorm beneath the Summit","artist":"Katsushika Hokusai; Publisher: Nishimuraya Yohachi","date":"1830–33","imageThumb":"https://img.artsmia.org/web_objects_cache/009000/600/10/9612/mia_7021688_800.jpg","imageHires":"https://img.artsmia.org/web_objects_cache/009000/600/10/9612/mia_7021688_full.jpg","license":"Public Domain","sourceUrl":"https://collections.artsmia.org/art/9612","accession":"74.1.227","medium":"Woodblock print (nishiki-e), ink and color on paper","dims":{"width":9278,"height":6431}},{"id":"aic:159360","source":"aic","nativeId":"159360","title":"Blue and Orange Sky","artist":"Paul Huet","date":"1838/40","imageThumb":"https://www.artic.edu/iiif/2/7bee86a4-cd58-3cdf-7208-981146f5e667/full/843,/0/default.jpg","imageHires":"https://www.artic.edu/iiif/2/7bee86a4-cd58-3cdf-7208-981146f5e667/full/1686,/0/default.jpg","license":"CC0","sourceUrl":"https://www.artic.edu/artworks/159360","accession":"2002.462","medium":"Pastel on tanned-gray wove paper with blue fibers","dims":{"width":3000,"height":2033},"color":{"h":41,"s":21,"l":50}}],"followUps":["Only calm skies","More fire, less storm","Just prints"],"comments":{"cma:141639":"Look at the red clouds shredded across the whole upper half, with teal holes torn through them. Cleveland's label notes Church showed it alone, and hundreds paid to see it. It reads as a Civil War omen.","aic:57996":"Here the sky is lit from underneath, with lava turning the smoke orange while a calm moon sits in the clouds on the right. The Art Institute notes the inscription: painted on the spot, 14 May 1771.","aic:109938":"Turner stops separating sky, mountain and avalanche. The whole picture spins into one storm, and the tiny figures at the bottom are barely there.","mia:9612":"The lightning is a flat orange zigzag below the peak, in a print where the sky above stays calm. That's a dramatic choice, and Minneapolis links it to Hokusai's thunder gods."}},"at":27808,"doneAt":27808}]}